  "derivation": "There is no Old Persian *naktin — indeed no Old Persian reflex of *nokʷt- at all. The Achaemenid inscriptions use xšap- 'night' (DB I 20 xšapavā raučapativā 'either by night or by day'); Kent, Old Persian §88 lists 'xšap- \"night\"' among the p-stems, and Buck 14.42 gives 'Av., OPers. xšap-' for 'night', remarking that in Indo-Iranian, apart from Vedic relics, 'the old word was displaced by others'. The form in the box comes from Nourai's own source, but there it is OLD PRUSSIAN: Mann, IEC 850 s.v. noktis, nokts writes 'I-stem in Skt. (Vedic) náktih, f.; OPr. naktin, acc. sg.' — the item immediately after the Sanskrit form that fills the next box of this very chart — and his separate entry noktím reads 'OPr. naktin \"by night\"'. Mann's abbreviation for Persian is 'Per.' (cf. 'Per. cf. na-γam' at IEC 364), so 'OPr.' cannot be Persian. Walde-Pokorny II 338 has the same form as 'apr. naktin (Akk. Sg.) \"Nacht\"' (apr. = altpreußisch) and Pokorny 762 as 'Old Prussian naktin (Akk. Sg.)'. Wiktionary's *nókʷts lists no Old Persian descendant at all. The box is therefore a misreading of 'OPr.' as 'Old Persian', promoting an Old Prussian accusative singular to an Old Persian lemma. The JSON matches the printed page, so this is the author's error, not the extraction's.",
