  "derivation": "Greek νύξ, gen. νυκτός, dat. νυκτί 'night'; the compositional/oblique stem νυκτι-/νυκτο- is Nourai's 'nukti'. Pokorny 762: 'gr. νύξ, νυκτός f. \"night\", in compound νυκτι-, νυκτο-, νύκτωρ adv. \"at night, by night\"'. Phonetically κτ is the regular Greek outcome of *kʷt; the vocalism υ instead of the expected ο is the crux — Buck calls it 'prob. fr. a reduced grade' and Mann suspects an original *noqutis with u-affection ('ultimately *noqutis, *noquís (?) in view of the vocalism of Gk. nux and Li. nakvóti'), while the modern account takes the u as the labial residue of the lost labiovelar; the same υ appears in γυμνός at entry 0 node #11 and, in the accepted Hittite comparison, in neku- 'to become night'.",
